==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{proto|Germanic|swīkanan|lang=ang}}. Cognate with Old Frisian ''swīka'' ‘stay far from’, Old Saxon ''swīkan'' ‘betray, languish’ (Dutch ''[[bezwijken]]'' ‘give way, sink’), Old High German ''swīhhan'' (dialectical German ''[[schweichen]]'' ‘wander round, deceive’), Old Norse ''[[svíkva]]'' ‘betray’ (Swedish ''[[svika]]'', Danish ''[[svige]]'').

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈswiːkɑn/|lang=ang}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|type=strong|head=swīcan|class=I}}

# to [[deceive]], be treacherous
